# FEX on Aurora

This is FEX built from Aurora's fork (https://github.com/aurora-silicon/fex),
packaged as fex-emu. It replaces the FEX-Emu package from the Asahi ALARM
repository, so `pacman -S fex-emu` and `pacman -S FEX-Emu` both install it.

## 4 KiB pages

FEX needs a 4 KiB page size. Aurora kernels use 16 KiB pages but can give a
process tree 4 KiB pages when the kernel is booted with `user4k=on` (the
default on Aurora images; `aurora-userpages` adds it to the GRUB command line).
Start x86 programs inside a 4 KiB tree:

    userpages 4k FEXBash
    userpages 4k FEX ./program

Programs started from a 4 KiB tree inherit it, including x86 programs that
binfmt_misc hands to FEX. Started from an ordinary 16 KiB process,
FEX runs with 16 KiB pages and x86 programs fail. `userpages --status` shows
the current page size. Aurora's Steam launcher starts Steam in a 4 KiB tree.

## Hardware TSO

On kernels that offer the memory-model prctl (PR_SET_MEM_MODEL), FEX switches
the process to the hardware total store order mode and drops its software
TSO emulation. Check with:

    FEXGetConfig --tso-emulation-info

## x86 parity and adjust flags

Apple cores can compute the x86 parity and adjust flags, PF and AF, in
hardware. When the kernel advertises this extension, called APFLG, FEX turns
it on at startup and keeps PF and AF in the host flags instead of computing
them in software. `FEX_HOSTFEATURES=disableapflg` turns it off.

## Root filesystem

FEX needs an x86-64 root filesystem for anything but static programs. The
global config, /usr/share/fex-emu/Config.json, sets RootFS to
/usr/share/fex-emu/RootFS/default.erofs, the image from fex-emu-rootfs-arch
(a dependency of fex-emu). FEXServer mounts the image with erofsfuse when FEX
starts, and runs erofsfuse with native pages (a 4 KiB page process can't
serve a FUSE mount on Aurora's kernels), so `userpages 4k FEXBash` works
without setup. To see the RootFS in use:

    userpages 4k FEXGetConfig --current-rootfs

If the image is missing, FEX's error names the package to install:

    sudo pacman -S fex-emu-rootfs-arch

Your own config, ~/.config/fex-emu/Config.json (~/.fex-emu/Config.json if
that directory exists), and the FEX_ROOTFS environment variable override the
global RootFS and Aurora's x86 Mesa (below). A RootFS of your own carries its
own x86 Mesa; remove its RootFS entry from your config to go back to Aurora's.
A RootFS given as a name rather than a path is looked up in
~/.local/share/fex-emu/RootFS and /usr/share/fex-emu/RootFS.
`FEXRootFSFetcher` downloads other root filesystems and `FEXConfig` edits
your config. Both come with fex-emu here (Fedora puts them in fex-emu-utils);
FEXConfig also needs qt6-declarative and sdl2-compat.

## Aurora's x86 Mesa

The stock image carries Arch's x86 Mesa, which doesn't match Aurora's kernel
and host Mesa. mesa-aurora-fex-guest, built from the same Mesa commit as
mesa-aurora and in Aurora's images by default, lays Aurora's x86_64 and i686
Mesa over that image: aurora-fex-mesa.service mounts the result, the graphics
provider, read-only at /usr/share/guestos/aurora-fex-mesa. fex-emu mounts the
image at /usr/share/guestos/fex-mesa as its lower layer; if the image arrives
after the provider came up without it, fex-emu mounts the provider again over
the image. Steam's FEX titles get the provider from Steam; their root
filesystem is the Steam runtime.

mesa-aurora-fex-guest is optional for fex-emu, so pacman doesn't bring it
back once removed. To keep it installed but run x86 programs on the stock
image, disable the provider (Steam's FEX titles then lose it too):

    sudo systemctl disable --now aurora-fex-mesa.service

or, for one user only, name the stock image in your own config:

    { "Config": { "RootFS": "/usr/share/fex-emu/RootFS/default.erofs" } }

While the provider is mounted, the other x86 programs FEX runs use it too
(FEXBash and programs that binfmt_misc hands to FEX). After the service mounts
it, /usr/lib/fex-emu/aurora-fex-rootfs bind-mounts it read-only at
/run/fex-emu/rootfs and writes /run/fex-emu/Config.json, a runtime config that
FEX reads over the global one:

    {
      "Config": {
        "RootFS": "/run/fex-emu/rootfs"
      }
    }

It writes the file only if the provider has Aurora's Mesa layer, the image's
x86-64 dynamic loader below it, and the layer's x86-64 Vulkan driver in the
same directory, which needs a layer laid out for Arch's directories
(x86_64 in usr/lib, i686 in usr/lib32). When the service stops, the provider
can't be used, or the file can't be written in full (a full /run), the file
goes and FEX uses the stock image through erofsfuse as above; the service's
journal says why. The provider is a kernel mount, so FEXServer starts no
FUSE helper for it, and it works from a 4 KiB page tree as the image does.

FEX runs from the bind, not from the provider's mount point, so the provider
can be unmounted for an upgrade of mesa-aurora-fex-guest while x86 programs
run. Those programs keep the mount they started on until they exit, but, as
with any library upgrade, files they open afterwards can come from the new
build. Programs started afterwards use the new provider. A bind still in use
stays mounted below the new one, and goes at the next change once it is idle.
A FEXServer keeps the RootFS it started with until its last x86 program exits.

## Thunks

This build has no host library thunks (as the FEX-Emu package it replaces).
